export default {"id":"drums-level-3","number":3,"title":"Four on the Floor","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the drums textbook","summary":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes The \"four-on-the-floor\" metric pattern is the historical foundation of modern club music. While this curriculum specializes heavily in Dubstep (~140 BPM Half-Time) and Drum & Bass (~174 BPM Breakbeats), ","modules":[{"id":"drums-m3-1","code":"3.1","title":"Four-on-the-Floor Literacy — House Beat Mechanics & Genre Contrasts","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes The \"four-on-the-floor\" metric pattern is the historical foundation of modern club music. While this curriculum specializes heavily in Dubstep (~140 BPM Half-Time) and Drum & Bass (~174 BPM Breakbeats), ","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-3-1","title":"Section 3.1 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The \"four-on-the-floor\" metric pattern is the historical foundation of modern club music. While this curriculum specializes heavily in Dubstep (~140 BPM Half-Time) and Drum &amp; Bass (~174 BPM Breakbeats), mastering the basic 4/4 house pattern is required for basic rhythmic literacy and serves as the baseline contrast against syncopated and half-time genres."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Construct</strong> an authentic four-on-the-floor house rhythm matrix at 120–130 BPM utilizing TR-909 kick, snare/clap, and open/closed hi-hats.","<strong>Execute</strong> standard backbeat snare placement on Beats 2 and 4 (Steps 5 and 13) and offbeat open hi-hats on all upbeat eighth notes (Steps 3, 7, 11, 15).","<strong>Articulate</strong> the exact metric and structural distinctions between a four-on-the-floor pattern and a 140 BPM Dubstep half-time pattern.","<strong>Articulate</strong> the exact metric and structural distinctions between a four-on-the-floor pattern and a 174 BPM Drum &amp; Bass breakbeat.","<strong>Pass</strong> Gate Assessment A (<code>drums-A</code>)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l3-1-3","title":"3.1.3: Why This is Not Dubstep","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.3: Why This is Not Dubstep","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Understanding why a four-on-the-floor pattern fails to produce a Dubstep groove is essential for genre mastery:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------------------------+      +------------------------------------+\n|         HOUSE MUSIC (4/4)          |      |         DUBSTEP (140 HALF-TIME)    |\n|------------------------------------|      |------------------------------------|\n| • Tempo: 120 - 130 BPM             |      | • Tempo: 140.00 BPM                |\n| • Kick on every beat (1, 5, 9, 13) |      | • Kick primarily on Beat 1 (Step 1)|\n| • Snare on Beats 2 &amp; 4 (5 &amp; 13)    |      | • Snare strictly on Beat 3 (Step 9)|\n| • High kinetic continuous pulse    |      | • Massive negative space for sub   |\n| • Perceived pulse = 125 BPM        |      | • Perceived pulse = 70 BPM         |\n+------------------------------------+      +------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Two Critical Distinctions:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Snare Coordinate Displacement:</strong> Moving the snare from Steps 5 and 13 to <strong>Step 9</strong> immediately destroys the 4/4 dancefloor pulse and creates the half-time drop identity.","<strong>Kick Saturation vs. Low-End Cavity:</strong> Placing four kicks per bar saturates the low-frequency spectrum, leaving no acoustic room for prolonged sub-bass wobble modulations (40–60 Hz). Dubstep removes the kicks on Beats 2, 3, and 4 to clear space for the bass drop."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l3-1-4","title":"3.1.4: Why This is Not Drum & Bass","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.4: Why This is Not Drum & Bass","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Similarly, four-on-the-floor differs fundamentally from Drum &amp; Bass:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------------------------+      +------------------------------------+\n|         HOUSE MUSIC (4/4)          |      |         DRUM &amp; BASS (174)          |\n|------------------------------------|      |------------------------------------|\n| • Tempo: 120 - 130 BPM             |      | • Tempo: 174 - 175 BPM             |\n| • Even, symmetric kick distribution|      | • Syncopated, broken kick pattern  |\n| • Kick on 1, 5, 9, 13              |      | • Kick on 1 and offbeat 11/12      |\n| • Snare on 5 and 13                |      | • Snare on 5 and 13 (High Velocity)|\n| • Linear 4-beat drive              |      | • Rolling syncopated break dynamic |\n+------------------------------------+      +------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Two Critical Distinctions:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Metric Velocity Delta:</strong> DnB operates +39.2% faster (174 BPM vs. 125 BPM). 16th notes fly by at 86.2 ms intervals rather than 120 ms.","<strong>Broken Kick Syncopation:</strong> DnB never places kicks on all four beats. It anchors Kick 1 on Step 1, but displaces Kick 2 to <strong>Step 11 (Beat 3.3)</strong> or <strong>Step 12 (Beat 3.4)</strong>, creating the signature syncopated breakbeat stumble and momentum."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 3.1: House Beat Construction & Transformation","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Ableton Live 12. Create a MIDI Track with a <strong>Drum Rack</strong> loaded with standard 909 samples.","Set project tempo to <code>125.00 BPM</code>.","Construct the 1-bar House Pattern:","Kicks: Steps 1, 5, 9, 13 (Velocity: 118, 112, 115, 112).","Claps: Steps 5, 13 (Velocity: 115, 120).","Open Hats: Steps 3, 7, 11, 15 (Velocity: 105).","Closed Hats: Steps 1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16 (Velocity: 85).","Verify playback. Confirm that the offbeat open hats and backbeat claps create a driving 4-on-the-floor groove.","<strong>Transformation Exercise:</strong>","Change project tempo to <code>140.00 BPM</code>.","Delete kicks on Steps 5, 9, 13.","Delete clap on Step 5. Move clap on Step 13 to <strong>Step 9</strong>.","Listen to the immediate transition into a half-time Dubstep skeleton."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Gate Assessment A Checklist (drums-A)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before proceeding to Module 4 (Stock Drum Devices), verify the following self-assessment criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] A 1-bar 4-on-the-floor house pattern exists in your DAW at 120–130 BPM.","[ ] Kick triggers on Steps 1, 5, 9, 13.","[ ] Snare or Clap triggers on Steps 5 and 13.","[ ] Open Hi-Hat triggers on Steps 3, 7, 11, 15.","[ ] You can explain in one sentence why moving the snare from Step 5 to Step 9 transforms the meter into half-time."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>What are the exact 16-step coordinates for a standard four-on-the-floor kick pattern?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Steps 1, 5, 9, and 13.","<strong>On which step coordinates do offbeat open hi-hats land in a classic house groove?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Steps 3, 7, 11, and 15 (the upbeat eighth notes).","<strong>What single MIDI transformation converts a 4/4 house snare pattern into a Dubstep half-time snare pattern?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Muting the snare on Step 5 (Beat 2) and shifting the second snare from Step 13 (Beat 4) to Step 9 (Beat 3)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":20}],"exercises":[]}]};
